# Scope guard for the automated AUR bump.
#
# The nightly `aur-bump.yml` workflow opens a PR that the vetting routine may
# merge without a human. Everything outside `aur/` therefore requires the repo
# owner's review: a bump that somehow reached Containerfile.base or a workflow
# would need an approval the automation cannot give itself. Last matching
# pattern wins, and an entry with no owner clears ownership.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO. CODEOWNERS matches on path, never on author, so the
# `/aur/` exemption below is equally open to a pull request from a stranger --
# this repository is public and anyone can open one touching only `aur/`. This
# file is not what stops that. The API caller checks eligibility in code before
# starting the routine. Among the checks is this combination
# that an ordinary outside contributor cannot satisfy:
#   - the PR author is `github-actions[bot]`
#   - the head repository is `gablank/boxes` (a fork can never satisfy this,
#     and pushing a branch inside the repo needs write access)
#   - the PR carries the `aur-bump` label (labelling needs triage/write access)
# Those are checked in ordinary code before any diff is sent to Claude.
#
# The caller sends only a validated zero-context diff and fixed identifiers.
# The prompt forbids reading other content or changing labels, but does not
# restrict the available tools or GitHub identity permissions. The merge-time gate is the
# `aur-bump/eligible` commit status that `aur-bump.yml` posts on the branch it
# creates: mark that context as a REQUIRED STATUS CHECK in branch protection and
# GitHub refuses a merge lacking it when the merging identity cannot bypass the
# rule. Other writers with status permission can post the same context; it is
# not exclusive to this workflow. The caller requires the GitHub Actions app
# binding; separately audit the merger's bypass and administration permissions.
#
# If you would rather not rely on unattended merging at all, delete the `/aur/`
# line. Every bump then needs your approval -- one click on a PR that already
# carries a written audit.
#
# INERT WITHOUT REPO SETTINGS. Nothing here applies until branch protection on
# `main` enables "Require a pull request before merging" + "Require review from
# Code Owners", and (for the status above) "Require status checks to pass" with
# `aur-bump/eligible` selected. A status-only ruleset does not enable Code Owner
# enforcement. Audit bypass rights explicitly: automation using the owner's
# identity can inherit the owner's bypass permissions.

